t all the remaining tiles are single. \n\nHmm. Maybe I should look for other combinations. Let's consider another approach. Suppose I form a Chi with T3-5, then maybe W4 and W6 can't form a Chi. What about the B2, B7, B9? Maybe B7 and B9 could be part of a sequence if there's a B8. But I don't have B8. So maybe that's not helpful. \n\nAlternatively, maybe there's a Pung in the hand. But all the remaining tiles are single. So no. \n\nWait, maybe I can form a Pung with B7 or B9? But I only have one each. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps the best way is to form a Chi T3-5, then look for another Chi. Let's see, if I have T3-5, then the remaining tiles are W4, W6, B2, B7, B9. Is there a way to form another Chi here? W4 and W6 are in the same suit. If I can get W5, that would form a Chi. But I don't have W5. So maybe not. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can discard one of the tiles to form a pair. Let's see. For example, if I discard B7, then B9 is left. But there's no pair. If I discard B9, same. If I discard B2, then no. \n\nAlternatively, maybe there's a different way to arrange the melds. Let's see. Suppose I don't form the T3-5 Chi. Maybe there's another way. Let's check the other tiles. \n\nWhat if I form a Pung with B2? No, I only have one B2. How about B7 or B9? No. \n\nWait, maybe there's a different combination. Let me check again. The hand has W4, W6, T3, T4, T5, B2, B7, B9. \n\nIf I can form a Chi of T3-5 (T3, T4, T5), that's one meld. Then the remaining tiles are W4, W6, B2, B7, B9. \n\nNow, I need another meld and a pair. Let's see if there's a pair. The remaining tiles are all single. So maybe I need to form another Chi. \n\nLooking at W4 and W6. If I can get W5, that would form a Chi. But I don't have W5. So maybe not. \n\nAlternatively, maybe B7 and B9 can be part of a sequence if there's a B8. But I don't have B8. \n\nHmm. This seems tricky. Maybe I need to discard a tile to form a pair. Let's see. For example, if I discard B7, then B9 is left. No pair. If I discar