 hand. \n\nIf I discard B7, then I have B9 left. If I can form a Chi with B7-9, but I don't have B8. So that's not possible. \n\nIf I discard B9, same problem. \n\nIf I discard B2, then I have B7 and B9 left. Still no meld. \n\nIf I discard T3, then I lose the possibility of the Chi T3-5. \n\nHmm. Maybe the best option is to discard a tile that's not part of a potential meld. Since B2 is a single tile and not part of any sequence, discarding it might be the safest. \n\nAlternatively, maybe B7 or B9. Let's see. If I discard B7, then the remaining B9 is still in the hand. But B9 is a terminal tile. Maybe others need it. \n\nBut in terms of my own hand, discarding B7 would leave me with B9, which is a terminal. Maybe that's okay. \n\nAlternatively, if I discard B9, same logic. \n\nBut I need to check which discard would allow me to form the most melds. \n\nIf I discard B7, then the remaining tiles are W4, W6, T3, T4, T5, B2, B9. I can form the Chi T3-5, but then I still need another meld and a pair. \n\nSame if I discard B9. \n\nIf I discard B2, then the remaining tiles are W4, W6, T3, T4, T5, B7, B9. Again, same problem. \n\nSo maybe the best move is to discard a tile that's not part of any potential meld. B2 is a good candidate. \n\nAlternatively, maybe W4 or W6. If I discard W4, then I have W6 left. If I can form a Chi with W6 and others? No, unless I get W5 and W7. But I don't have those. \n\nHmm. Maybe the best option is to discard B2, as it's a single tile not part of any sequence. \n\nSo the answer would be Play B2.\n", "output": "Evaluation: [The hand has potential for forming a Chi with T3, T4, T5. Discarding B2 would not interfere with this and allows forming necessary melds.]\nReason: [Discarding B2 preserves the T3-T4-T5 sequence for a Chi, which is crucial for completing the hand. Other tiles like B7/B9 are terminal tiles and may be needed for other melds or by opponents.]\nAnswer: Play B2"}
